---
This page sets `font  book` in its header. The name is just a filename: drop
`book.woff2` into `site/fonts/` and `/fonts.css` picks it up on the next
request. No registration, no config, no build step.

## per word, not just per page

Inline, `{class|text}` wraps anything in a span. Every font in the folder also
gets an `f-` class automatically, so a single word can change face:

{f-grotesk|grotesk} · {f-serif|serif} · {f-mono|mono} · {f-book|book} ·
{f-humanist|humanist} · {f-quran|quran}

The same mechanism reaches everything else CSS can do:

{huge|huge} · {tiny|tiny} · {wide-track|w i d e} · {tight|tight} ·
{smallcaps|small caps} · {invert|inverted} · {outline|outline} ·
{faint|faint} · {upside|upside}

You can ==highlight== a phrase, make it **strong**, make it *emphatic*, or nest
them: {f-mono|{invert|nested spans work}}.

## adding your own

Any class you define in a skin is immediately usable as `{myclass|word}`. The
markup does not need to know the class exists — it just writes the span and
lets CSS decide. That is the whole freedom mechanism: one syntax, unbounded
vocabulary.

> Six faces here are subsets of system fonts, 12–20KB each, cut to Latin-1 with
> `pyftsubset`. The whole folder is 84KB. An unsubset TTF is often 700KB on its
> own.

See [[colophon]] for the subsetting command.
